[Music]  i'm  often drawn to very generic materials as  a starting point for my work i think  because they have  a very easy entry point things feel  immediately identifiable in some way  um there's a material resonance i think  that  people feel that they understand what it  is  and  and in a way that  easy entry then i feel opens up the  possibility of  metaphors kind of expanding out from  them  the first time i started using  the materials that are in this  exhibition the  the pieces that look like figures i call  these forms slumps because they're sort  of these figurative forms that depend on  a hard structure of some kind to support  them an armature  and their way of thinking about  the relation between  the body and um and the support systems  that hold up the body  as a kind of code event codependent  relationship  and i was particularly interested in  foam as the kind of base material for  these  slumps because it is a material that's  used it's a sort of cheap bed  material the other material in this  exhibition of course is are the  emergency blankets  they are  of course  materials that are used to keep people  warm  it's it has a kind of protective  element to it  but when  stretched it also turns into a kind of  two-way mirror  i'm  interested in that and how as people  walk in front of and behind this  vertical plane  they are hidden and revealed  from each other  they are reflected again into the space  and sometimes into each other because  it's a two-way mirror and it also  presents the sculpture  through reflection  in this rounded form  so there are a variety of formal and  conceptual reasons that are always  working together in my material choices  and i'm always thinking about these  different  sort of  layers of the space vertically  and in this exhibition there are  hanging um  water bottles  that are sort of at mid-level they're  kind of at eye level um  they are these uh i think of them as  extensions of the body they're water  bottles so they are used to carry water  which you drink  to um to kind of  replenish your own water but i've also  been interested in how  day laborers and people who again are  don't have space don't have a bathroom  to go to often will use that same bottle  to  urinate and so it becomes this  receptacle for bodily fluids as well and  and so in a way it's a and it's a size  of a head and it hangs at head height so  i think of them as again bodily  at this at the height of a person  standing  and then finally these exit signs or  pieces that are quite high up on the  wall and force people to look up  they're also hung in a way where from  any  given point you can  at best see one side of an exit sign and  so you have to move through the space to  see  the two words on any given sign and so  the movement of  the viewer is an important part of the  piece things are hidden and revealed as  you walk through the space  in this current show and in previous  shows i've used text in different ways  here it's  through these what i think of as  two-word poems in a way these exit signs  that  i think help people  contextualize  the work  gives people anchors through which to  enter the work rather than  provide a kind of descriptive  entry point  so  for example let's see grape grove the  title of the exhibition  which is also an exit sign  in the in the exhibition  um  was a way of thinking about  this moment that we're living through  the pandemic  as a moment that is of course one that  reminds us of our mortality we're  confronted constantly with death  but last spring um  there was this moment when  the sort of the  seasons changed in new york and plans  started to kind of grow out of the  sidewalk cracks and there was something  quite beautiful about this  rebirth  within a city  a rebirth not so much of the people but  of this other  life form these plants and particularly  plants that  are not considered valuable to humans  that are considered weeds but that  irrespective of our desire to have them  around they thrive and they  come back every year  [Music]  you

